WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.759
Temporary file security means treating short-lived files and directories
as real storage with real security impact.

2
00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:20.278
Python applications create temporary artifacts during uploads, report
generation, document conversion, archive handling, image processing,
tests, data pipelines, background jobs, command-line tools, and subprocess
workflows.

3
00:00:20.278 --> 00:00:29.336
These files may exist for seconds or minutes, but while they exist they
can contain sensitive data, control application behavior, or affect later
processing.

4
00:00:29.336 --> 00:00:37.380
Temporary files are different from caches, logs, uploads, working
directories, and persistent storage, but the boundaries often overlap.

5
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.827
A temporary upload may later become a permanent attachment.

6
00:00:40.827 --> 00:00:43.058
A report may be staged before download.

7
00:00:43.058 --> 00:00:46.302
A converted image may be passed through several workers.

8
00:00:46.302 --> 00:00:49.141
A temporary directory may hold archive contents.

9
00:00:49.141 --> 00:00:54.752
Because temporary files sit between steps, teams sometimes treat them as
harmless scratch space.

10
00:00:54.752 --> 00:00:56.577
That habit is where risk begins.

11
00:00:56.577 --> 00:01:07.257
A temporary file can contain user content, personal data, generated
documents, intermediate outputs, private business records, decrypted data,
credentials, or operational details.

12
00:01:07.257 --> 00:01:18.815
It can also affect integrity if another process can modify it,
availability if cleanup fails and disk fills, and auditability if nobody
can explain where a sensitive artifact came from or why it remained.

13
00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:24.561
Short lifetime reduces exposure only when creation, access, and deletion
are actually controlled.

14
00:01:24.561 --> 00:01:28.549
Secure temporary storage should be designed rather than improvised.

15
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:46.529
The application should choose safe APIs, avoid manual predictable names,
use controlled directories, apply appropriate permissions, minimize
sensitive data written to disk, handle cleanup after success and failure,
and test file workflows that involve uploads, archives, subprocesses, and
background jobs.

16
00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:48.692
Temporary does not mean unimportant.

17
00:01:48.692 --> 00:01:52.680
It means the lifecycle should be deliberately short and well managed.
